# smap-uaccess.manifest - #645 ledger of Ring-0 accesses to USER memory that do
# NOT go through the copy_*_user primitives.
#
# FORMAT   <file>:<line>: <substring that must still be present near that line>
# SECTIONS EXEMPT    provably safe for a stated reason; not a fault under SMAP.
#          KNOWN_GAP a REAL unwrapped user access. Under SMAP this is a #PF on
#                    a path the kernel takes in normal operation. Listed so the
#                    gate can go green on "nothing NEW landed" while never
#                    implying the tree is clean.
#
# THE LEDGER IS CHECKED, NOT TRUSTED. smap-uaccess-lint rule B1 re-reads every
# entry and FAILS if the substring is no longer near that line: a site that was
# fixed MUST be delisted, and a site that moved MUST have its line corrected.
# This is the #514 lesson applied to a ledger: a list of things that used to be
# true is worse than no list, because it reads as coverage.
#
# THE INTERLOCK. While this file has ANY gap entry, the lint FAILS a build whose
# SMAP compile-time default is ON (i.e. one built without -DCONFIG_NO_SMAP).
# Enabling SMAP with sites unconverted does not produce a hardened kernel, it
# produces one that #PFs during process launch. Drain the ledger first; the
# interlock is what stops that from being a judgement call.
#
# ===========================================================================
# #19 (2026-08-11): THE LEDGER WAS DRAINED TO ONE ENTRY, AND CR4.SMAP IS STILL
# OFF BECAUSE OF THAT ONE ENTRY. Read the gap section before touching the flag.
# ===========================================================================
# What the old ledger said, and what was actually true when it was re-measured
# against the tree at f5652a4:
#
#   * It carried 125 gap entries and kernel/Makefile summarised them as "126
#     measured Ring-0 accesses to user memory". That number was WRONG IN BOTH
#     DIRECTIONS.
#     - About 35 entries had ALREADY BEEN FIXED, by #566/#567/#700, and nobody
#       delisted them. They anchor on the DECLARATION line of a user pointer
#       (`char *ub = (char *)arg1;`), which rule B3 flags, while the write two
#       lines below had become a copy_to_user(). Rule B1 only checks that the
#       anchor TEXT is still present, and a declaration does not move when the
#       body under it is fixed, so a fixed site reads exactly like a live one.
#     - About 25 entries were never user accesses at all. `process_t *p =
#       proc_current();` and `p->brk = new_brk;` are kernel-struct accesses that
#       B3 attributes to a user pointer because they share a function with one.
#       Those are below, in the exempt section, with the reason stated.
#     - And it MISSED real sites: drivers/tty.c's tty_output_drain(),
#       drivers/usb_cdc_acm.c's write-direction twin, and the whole IPC name
#       service (ipc/msg.c sys_ipc_register_name/sys_ipc_lookup_name, added
#       after the audit). Its prose UNDETECTABLE section had also gone stale on
#       line numbers: fs/fat.c 1312/2337 -> 1841/3026, drivers/dev.c 98/143/151
#       -> 99/145/153.
#   * The genuinely unwrapped sites it DID name are converted: either to a
#     copy_*_user() / strncpy_from_user() call, or to a uaccess_begin()/
#     uaccess_end() bracket placed around the ACCESS (a copy loop, a batch of
#     stores, a copy engine), never around a whole function.
#   * And arming CR4.SMAP exposed a class NEITHER the ledger nor the lint could
#     see, which is the single remaining blocker. See the gap section below.
#
# The conversions were verified by BOOTING an armed kernel, not by reading this
# lint's output. The first armed boot died at RIP=0x5d3056 (memcpy_fast)
# CR2=0x80000000 err=0x3, which is exec/elf.c's PT_LOAD copy: the ledger's own
# headline claim, confirmed on a machine.
#
# HONEST LIMIT, unchanged: this lint cannot prove the ABSENCE of an unwrapped
# access. An empty gap section means no site of the recognised shapes is left,
# not that the tree is provably clean. The boot is the other half of the proof.

[EXEMPT]

# --- #120: THE TWO `_k` KERNEL-POINTER CORES (#567). ------------------------
# These 11 sites became VISIBLE at #120, and they were invisible for a reason
# that was a defect, not a policy: rule B3's copy_from_user/copy_to_user/
# strncpy_from_user seeds used a GREEDY `[^,()]*`, so the capture group took the
# letters LEFT OVER after the identifier was swallowed. `copy_to_user(ubuf, ..)`
# seeded the name `f`; `copy_from_user(&k, uptr, n)` seeded `r`. The scan then
# watched names that do not exist and never watched the real buffer, and its
# helper-following (GAP 2) could not follow a call it could not name. Fixed at
# #120 (see the comment at the seed, and the two new self-test cases that go RED
# on the old regex), which is what surfaced these.
#
# They are EXEMPT, not KNOWN_GAP, and the distinction is the point. sys_open_k()
# and sys_http_post_k() are the KERNEL-POINTER halves of the #567 split: the
# user-facing wrapper (sys_open / sys_http_post) bounces EVERY user pointer into
# a kernel buffer with strncpy_from_user/copy_from_user and only then calls the
# core, and each core carries an in-code comment saying exactly that. Their
# in-kernel callers (sys_chdir, the #308/#317 boot self-tests, the RC/shell
# HTTP path) pass kernel pointers directly.
#
# What the lint sees is `sys_open_k(upath ? kpath : (const char *)0, flags)`:
# the argument TEXT mentions the user pointer `upath`, in the condition, while
# the value actually passed is the bounced `kpath`. Distinguishing those needs
# dataflow, which this tool states plainly that it does not do. That is the
# textbook case for this section: safe for a documented reason the regex cannot
# model.
proc/fdlayer.c:607: if (path && path[0]
proc/fdlayer.c:753: { int z = 0; while (path[z] && z < 259)
proc/fdlayer.c:789: { int z = 0; while (path[z] && z < 259)
proc/fdlayer.c:833: if (g_root_ext2 && path && path[0]
proc/fdlayer.c:836: while (path[z] && z < 258)
proc/fdlayer.c:848: { int z = 0; while (rel[z] && z < 255)
proc/syscall.c:5214: if (net_fetch_blocked()) { if (status_out)
proc/syscall.c:5215: int https = (url[0]
proc/syscall.c:5220: if (r < 0) { if (rbody) kfree(rbody);
proc/syscall.c:5222: if (rbody && n) memcpy(kbuf, rbody, n);
proc/syscall.c:5214: if (status_out) *status_out = status;

# --- proc/procinfo.c : c_cstr_into() is the tail of the ROW BUILDERS, and the
# AC window for those is opened by the CALLER, around the whole builder call
# (sys_proc_handles / sys_svc_list). The builder IS this path's copy engine (see
# the file header: "every byte copied into the caller's buffer is copied by the
# Rust builders through exactly-sized slices"), so bracketing the engine is the
# same discipline mm/uaccess.asm applies to its own copy loop. c_cstr_into's
# OTHER caller, sys_svc_control(), passes a kernel buffer.
proc/procinfo.c:23: for (uint32_t i = 0; i < cap; i++) dst[i] = 0;
proc/procinfo.c:27: while (i < room && src[i]) { dst[i] = src[i]; i++; }
proc/procinfo.c:28: dst[i] = 0;

# --- proc/process.c : a DECLARATION, not a dereference. Rule B3's pointer
# pattern matches the declaration itself. The store two lines below is a
# copy_to_user() now, matching thread.c's already-converted twin.
proc/process.c:3881: uint32_t *ctid = me->clear_child_tid;

# --- proc/signal.c : a DECLARATION, not a dereference. Every field store that
# used to go through `frame` is built in a kernel-local sigframe_t and handed
# over with ONE copy_to_user().
proc/signal.c:139: sigframe_t *frame = (sigframe_t *)user_rsp;

# --- proc/syscall.c sys_list_users : `t` is the KERNEL user table returned by
# users_all(). B3 attributes it to the user side because the same function calls
# copy_to_user() on its (genuinely user) output buffer. Reading a kernel table
# is not a user access, and the one user write here is already a copy_to_user().
proc/syscall.c:427: user_entry_t *t = users_all(&n);
proc/syscall.c:430: if (!t[i].active) continue;
proc/syscall.c:436: for (k = 0; k < 63 && t[i].username[k]; k++) ke.username[k] = t[i].use
proc/syscall.c:438: for (k = 0; k < 63 && t[i].display_name[k]; k++) ke.display_name[k] =
proc/syscall.c:440: ke.uid = t[i].uid;
proc/syscall.c:441: ke.gid = t[i].gid;

# --- proc/syscall.c spawn_impl : `kargp` is the KERNEL array that
# copy_from_user() just filled FROM argv. B3 marks a copy_from_user DESTINATION
# as a user pointer as well as its source, so the kernel-array reads below it
# are flagged. The two genuine Ring-3 reads in this function (the argv pointer
# array, and each argument string) are a copy_from_user() and a
# strncpy_from_user() respectively.
proc/syscall.c:3461: char *kargp[64];
proc/syscall.c:3466: if (!kargp[i]) break;

# --- #112 proc/syscall.c sys_spawn_env : the same B3 attribution artefact as
# `kargp` directly above, in the same function's new sibling.
#
# `uenv` is the KERNEL array that copy_from_user() just filled FROM req.envp,
# and `kenvp` is a KERNEL array of pointers into `ebuf`, which is kmalloc'ed.
# Reading either is a kernel read. The three GENUINE Ring-3 accesses in this
# function are all wrapped: copy_from_user() for the request struct,
# copy_from_user() for the envp pointer array, and validate_user_string() +
# strncpy_from_user() for each entry string.
proc/syscall.c:3738: char *kenvp[SC_ENV_MAX_ENTRIES + 1];
proc/syscall.c:3749: char *uenv[SC_ENV_MAX_ENTRIES];
proc/syscall.c:3757: if (!uenv[i]) break;
proc/syscall.c:3769: kenvp[kenvc] = ebuf[kenvc];
proc/syscall.c:3773: kenvp[kenvc] = NULL;

# --- #112 proc/syscall.c spawn_impl / open_redir_file : `path`, `infile` and
# `outfile` became VISIBLE to B3 for the first time at #112, and the reason is
# worth stating because it is not a new fault.
#
# B3 follows a copy_from_user() destination into the callees it is passed to.
# Before #112 the three paths reached spawn_impl() as raw dispatcher longs
# (`(const char *)arg1`), which B3 has no seed for, so it never watched them.
# sys_spawn_env() bounces its request struct with copy_from_user() and then
# passes req.path / req.infile / req.outfile out of it, which taints all three
# and, through spawn_impl's own call, open_redir_file's `path` too.
#
# THE DEREFERENCES THEMSELVES ARE OF THE BOUNCED KERNEL COPIES, NOT OF RING 3.
# spawn_impl's FIRST action on all three is sc_path_from_user() into kspath /
# kin / kout, and it then reassigns each parameter to its kernel copy (#58 put
# that chokepoint there for exactly this reason). Every line below runs after
# that reassignment, so what is being dereferenced is kernel memory.
#
# This is EXEMPT rather than KNOWN_GAP because the safety is a property of the
# code, not an outstanding fault: the reassignment is what the lint cannot see,
# which is the documented limit of a tool that states it does no dataflow.
proc/syscall.c:3568: if (outfile && outfile[0]) {
proc/syscall.c:3573: if (infile && infile[0]) {
proc/syscall.c:3312: if (!path || !path[0]) return NULL;

